          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A Cauchy real number is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real number
            that is given as the limit of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+sequence"} Cauchy sequence
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
            %em definition
            of the general concept of real number. This is due to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Cantor"} Georg Cantor
            in 1872, the same year that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Dedekind"} Richard Dedekind
            developed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+cuts"} Dedekind cuts
            as a definition of the same concept.
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            If we simply want a construction of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            for the purposes of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalisation"} internalise
            the Cauchy real line, then there are subtler alternatives.
          %h3#classical_version Classical version
          %p
            Putting Cantor's definition in modern terminology,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+set"} quotient set
            of the set of Cauchy sequences of rational numbers, with two sequences considered equivalent if their difference converges to zero. Although the notion of Cauchy sequence (and convergence, for that matter) is best known in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+spaces"} metric spaces
            (which cannot be defined in general without having, at least implicitly, constructed
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            already), it is easy to state the definitions in elementary terms. (We can also view these as Cauchy sequences in a
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Booij+premetric+space"} Booij premetric space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mn 0
