            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
            on the set of Cauchy sequences, so the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            of real numbers is a quotient set.
          %p
            This definition comes in two steps: one to identify the Cauchy sequences from among the infinite sequences, another to identify equivalent sequences. Actually, we can do this in one step by placing a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+equivalence+relation"} partial equivalence relation
            on the set of
            %em all
            infinite sequences of rational numbers. Two sequences
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,y
            are considered equivalent if, for every positive rational number
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
